#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175621 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0e2a8cf2-64db-5b2f-b0f7-0c727cf492a9" :knowledge-point/title "Describing Properties of the Secant Function"}] :question/id #uuid "06828f66-a8be-59f5-b82d-afd93333f38d" :question/is-example true :question/math-academy-id "e-11006" :question/problem "Which of the following statements regarding the graph of $y=\\sec x$ are true?\n\n1. The function takes the value $1$ at $x = 2n\\pi$, where $n$ is an integer\n2. The range of the function is $(-\\infty,+\\infty)$\n3. The function has zeros at $x = 0, \\pm \\pi, \\pm 2 \\pi,\\dots$" :question/worked-solution "Let's have a look at the graph of $y=\\sec x$.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3563/Source/Images/6d04c6687b88309d564d8222e10787db.png)\n\nLet's go through each statement.\n\n- The secant takes the value $1$ at $x = 2 n\\pi$. Therefore, statement I is true.\n- The range of $f(x) = \\sec x$ is $[MATH: (-\\infty,-1] \\cup [1, \\infty)]$. Therefore, statement II is false.\n- The secant graph does not intersect the $x$-axis. Therefore, statement III is false.\n\nIn conclusion, only statement I is true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175622 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ce45704e-ecea-5c67-bb40-a413f8a0d72e" :knowledge-point/title "Utilizing the Periodicity Property of the Secant Function"}] :question/id #uuid "f76b3e8f-5580-5cb0-8d6c-85711c446602" :question/is-example true :question/math-academy-id "e-11007" :question/problem "If $\\sec x =2 \\sqrt 2$ then what is $\\sec(x+8\\pi)$?" :question/worked-solution "The period of $y = \\sec x$ is $2\\pi$. Therefore, for integer $n$ we have\n\n$$\n\\sec{x} = \\sec{(x+2n\\pi)}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $n=4$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\sec x &= \\sec (x + 2 \\cdot 4 \\cdot π) \\\\\n&= \\sec (x + 8π)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, if\n\n$$\n\\sec{x} = 2\\sqrt 2\n$$\n\nthen\n\n$$\n\\sec{(x+8\\pi)} = 2\\sqrt 2\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175629 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d4b378b0-30e5-59a8-b9f7-882b5e226a2e" :knowledge-point/title "Utilizing the Evenness Property of the Secant Function"}] :question/id #uuid "6bd67fa4-16b5-5505-bfc5-35a00f54e980" :question/is-example true :question/math-academy-id "e-11014" :question/problem "If $\\sec{x} = -\\dfrac{11}{6}$, then $\\sec(-x - 14\\pi) =$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the graph of\n\n$$\ny = \\sec{x}\n$$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3563/Source/Images/54de4a90efd6ebeccfdace7b62890f94.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we note the following:\n\n- The period of $\\sec{x}$ is $2\\pi$.\n- Our graph is symmetric about the $y$-axis. This means that $\\sec{x}$ is an *even* function, and we have\n$\\sec(-x) = \\sec{x}$.\n\nLet's now evaluate our expression:\n\n- Firstly, using the periodicity of $\\sec{x}$, we have\n$\\sec (-x - 14π)|= \\sec (-x - 2π \\cdot 7); = \\sec (-x)$.\n- Secondly, using the evenness of $\\sec{x}$, we have\n$\\sec (-x)|= \\sec x; =-\\frac{11}{6}$.\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\sec(-x - 14\\pi) = -\\dfrac{11}{6}\n$$"}]}
